    "description": "The back-office application of a ticketing platform renders an operator console as server-side HTML. For each screen the current code path is smeared across routing, an ASP.NET view and three partial classes: request parameters are read in the view, validation happens twice, the workflow service is called from the template, and the redirect target is assembled in the markup. Adding a filter field last sprint broke the export button because nobody knew which layer owned the query state. The team wants per-request handling that receives the parsed request, validates it, calls the workflow, chooses the next view, and puts the view data into a presentation model the template can render without logic. The decision to implement: how one component handles a single request through validation, workflow invocation and view selection.",
    "decision_statement": "Handle one incoming request end to end: parse, validate, invoke the workflow and select the view.",
    "notes": "Holdout family: front-controller stays acceptable if the reviewer prefers one shared entry point."
